---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const principles = [
  {
    title: "Consistency over intensity",
    body: "The best training programme is the one you are still doing in March. PaceStreak measures the thing that predicts results — showing up — rather than the thing that feels impressive on a single Tuesday.",
  },
  {
    title: "Ten seconds or it doesn't get logged",
    body: "Every extra field is a reason to skip logging when you're tired, and a skipped log is a broken streak. Detail is available; it is never required.",
  },
  {
    title: "Rest is part of the plan",
    body: "A tracker that punishes recovery days is teaching the wrong lesson. Streaks count against a weekly target you set, so a deload week is kept, not broken.",
  },
  {
    title: "Your data leaves with you",
    body: "Full JSON and CSV export from the first release. A training history you cannot take with you is not really yours.",
  },
  {
    title: "No tracking, no third parties",
    body: "This site loads nothing from anyone else — no analytics, no font CDN, no embedded widgets. That is enforced by a content security policy in production, not just intended.",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="About — PaceStreak"
  description="Why PaceStreak exists, the principles it is built on, and how it is being built in the open."
  path="/about"
>
  <section class="py-16 sm:py-20">
    <div class="wrap max-w-3xl">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-5xl">
        Built for the Tuesday in February.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted text-pretty">
        Most fitness apps are built for the day you feel great. PaceStreak is
        built for the other days — the ones that actually decide whether you are
        still training in six months.
      </p>

      <div class="prose-page mt-14">
        <h2>The idea</h2>
        <p>
          Streaks work because they change the question. Instead of "do I feel
          like training today", it becomes "am I willing to be the person who
          breaks a 47 day run". That is a much easier question to answer well,
          and it does not depend on motivation being available.
        </p>
        <p>
          The grid is the other half. Six months of filled squares is a harder
          thing to abandon than a list of workouts, because it is visibly{" "}
          <strong>yours</strong> — a receipt for work already done rather than a
          promise about work to come.
        </p>

        <h2>What it is not</h2>
        <p>
          It is not a coaching app, a macro tracker, or a social network. There
          is no feed, no leaderboard, and nothing to compare yourself against
          except your own history. Those products exist and some of them are
          good; this one is deliberately smaller.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Principles</h2>
      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
        {
          principles.map((principle) => (
            <article class="card">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold">{principle.title}</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-[0.96rem] leading-relaxed text-muted">
                {principle.body}
              </p>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <div class="prose-page mt-16">
        <h2>Built in the open</h2>
        <p>
          PaceStreak is being built in public, including the parts that go
          wrong. The <a href="https://blog.pacestreak.com">blog</a> covers
          what is being built and what broke while building it — the DNS records
          that returned errors, the deploys that silently served stale files,
          the security headers that blocked the site's own scripts.
        </p>
        <p>
          Uptime is public too, at <a href="https://status.pacestreak.com"
            >status.pacestreak.com</a
          >. Every repository is{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/PaceStreak">on GitHub</a> under AGPL-3.0.
        </p>

        <h2>Get in touch</h2>
        <p>
          One address, read by a person: <a href="mailto:hello@pacestreak.com"
            >hello@pacestreak.com</a
          >. Early access goes out in batches — mail that address to be in the
          next one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
